WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
Processes and services show what a Linux system is doing or was configured to do.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:14.660
During live response, running processes can reveal active services, user sessions, command lines, parent-child relationships, and unusual execution locations.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:23.040
Because live collection can change the system, analysts should record what commands or tools were used and understand that volatile evidence may disappear quickly.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:28.460
Systemd services are a major source of persistence and operational context on many distributions.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:36.760
Service unit files, timers, enabled states, service logs, and recent changes can show how software starts and under which account it runs.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:43.220
Older systems or specialized appliances may use init scripts, rc files, or custom startup mechanisms.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.880
User-level services may also exist and should not be overlooked when investigating account activity.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:01:01.020
Cron jobs, at, anacron, shell startup files, profile scripts, and application-specific schedulers may indicate legitimate automation or activity that requires investigation.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.080
The forensic question is not simply whether a scheduled task exists.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:11.060
The question is whether it fits the system role, the account, the timeline, and the known administrative process.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:21.440
Unusual binaries, unexpected paths, recently modified service definitions, odd permissions, and unfamiliar startup entries can be persistence clues at a defensive level.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.220
Analysts should avoid jumping straight to conclusions.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:30.200
A strange file may be a vendor tool, a local admin script, or an indicator of compromise.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:38.420
Correlate service configuration with logs, file metadata, package records, network activity, and case context before making a finding.
